<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valka is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, forming a twin town with the Estonian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valga">Valga</a>, with which it was one town until 1920. In 1917–1918 it was the seat of the Latvian Bolshevik government, Iskolat, and in 1917 the Latvian Provisional National Council was formed there. The border through the town was drawn by British arbitration in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valka.jpg" alt="Valka" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Kikos at Latvian Wikipedia. (Transfered by Flying Saucer) J. Sedols · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valkas_bazn%C4%ABca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Valka is a town and the centre of Valka Municipality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, along both banks of the river Pedele. Its German name is Walk and its Estonian counterpart is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valga">Valga</a>. The two towns are twins, separated by the national border but using the slogan One Town, Two Countries. With the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-agreement">Schengen Agreement</a> and the abolition of border controls between Estonia and Latvia in 2007, the border crossing-points were removed and roads and fences opened. A joint public bus service was planned to link the twin towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town of Walk was first mentioned in 1286, and from 1419 it was the seat of the Landtag of the Livonian Confederation. The Polish-Lithuanian king Stefan Batory granted it city rights in 1584. It gained importance only at the end of the 19th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidzeme">Vidzeme</a> teachers' seminary, founded by Jānis Cimze, operated there and an important railway junction developed. The first narrow-gauge railway line in the territory of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> ran from Valka to the Estonian city of Pärnu.</p><p>On 15 November 1917 the Latvian Provisional National Council made the decision in Valka to proclaim the independent Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, and the red-white-red flag of Latvia was raised there for the first time. The town became the subject of a dispute between the newly formed Latvian and Estonian states. On 1 July 1920 it was divided between them as a compromise, and the border was marked out by an international jury headed by the British colonel Stephen George Tallents.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It governed the part of Latvia not occupied by Germany, with its seat in <b>Valka</b> and later Cēsis.</blockquote><a href="/state/iskolat">Iskolat</a></li></ul></section>
